R3192 evidence summary - a failed state write takes the archive back
====================================================================
Board row claimed: R3191 next-window (0) MAIN - the second half of the
half-landing: compensate after os.replace fails.

PROBE (read-only, tmp/probe_R3192.py -> qa/probe-R3192.log, private temp
worlds, zero socket, zero network, zero live writes)
  Q1a after a replace failure the refusal read E_SF_STATE_WRITE with
      stage=after-write while the backup and the archive were on disk.
  Q1b the archive grew by 295 bytes and the state kept its bytes.
  Q1c the ledger face was ALREADY rewritten: rounds 7 -> 7 but ledger
      lines 7 -> 9 for a fold that never landed.
  Q1d truncating the archive to before_bytes and removing the backup
      restored the exact pre-fold bytes (sha 8628313c9b240d63), the R9002
      marker vanished and coverage returned to 7/7.
  Q1e the same window then folded clean (predicted_eq_actual True,
      live 12 -> 9, rounds still 7).
  Q1f WITHOUT the rollback the retry was refused by
      E_SF_MARKER_COLLISION - the window became unretryable, and the only
      escape (a re-fold claim) writes the same content twice.
  Q2  consumers: run() after-write proofs are reachable only on success
      (SF.fold raises first); closeout_gate never references these faces;
      logs_toolkit classifies state-preop-* names but does not require
      them to exist; state_fold.py had no truncate path.
  Q3  append_archive returned sha16 of the AFTER bytes; after a rollback
      that hash described nothing on disk (report e424b1feb7deef64 vs
      disk 1e461438e4efba63), while prefix_intact stayed True and meant
      nothing. The rollback therefore has to re-state the fact instead of
      reusing the report.

LANDED (src/os/state_fold.py)
  append_archive publishes before_sha16 (ARCHIVE_KEYS extended).
  rollback_archive(path, appended): deletes an archive this fold created,
  otherwise truncates to before_bytes and proves the restoration against
  before_sha16 (E_SF_ARCHIVE, after-write, if the read-back differs).
  fold(): the state-write failure arm now rolls the archive back, removes
  the backup, and raises E_SF_STATE_WRITE with stage='rolled-back' and
  after_write=False. When the rollback itself fails the stage stays
  'after-write' and the detail says the archive still carries the window -
  the half-landing is reported, never dressed up as a clean refusal.
  USAGE names the third stage.

CRITERIA
  AC-SFOLD14 (six arms) in src/os/test_state_fold_run.py, 17 -> 17 total.
  JUDGED-CHANGED, all three measured rather than assumed:
    AC-SFOLD12 replace arm stage after-write -> rolled-back;
    AC-SFOLD12 code-site count 2 -> 3 (the replace arm split in two);
    AC-SFOLD13 replace arm stage after-write -> rolled-back;
    AC-SFOLD11 after-write raise-site count 4 -> 5;
    AC-SF3 archive report keys follow ARCHIVE_KEYS, which now carries
    before_sha16.
  Two of my own arms went red first and were corrected by measurement, not
  by loosening: the AC-SFOLD14 window had to open at 2..9 (the first fold
  leaves a summary line at index 0, so 13..20 landed mid-round and the fold
  refused before the injection), and the state comparison had to be
  snapshotted before the retry rather than after it.

